  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 507 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday:\n\nHot and dry conditions are ongoing ahead of Hurricane Isaias \napproach to the Gulf coast. Breezy conditions combined with \nhot/dry conditions in place, there is a potential for fire weather\nconcerns through Friday for the northern portions of the area. \n\nHurricane Isaias continues to move northeast in the central Gulf,\neventually turning northward towards the AL/FL coastline over the\nnext 24 to 36 hours. Isaias has strengthened to 100mph sustained\nwinds with the satellite presentation improving and pressure \n(975mb) continuing to deepen. GOES East infrared imagery currently\nindicate some increasing shear but a deep burst of convection is  \ndeveloping on the western side. Center of circulation will remain\nmostly east of area, making landfall in the FL Panhandle just \neast of the AL/FL state line. This landfall location will keep \ngreatest impacts further east. However, tightening gradient on the\nnorthwest side of the decaying system will bring potential of \nwind gusts of 40-50 mph across the region. Several convective \nallowing models (REFS/HRRR) and probabilities (REFS probs >40mph) \nindicate potential of stronger gusts in east-central MS up the \nInterstate 59 into Highway 45 corridor and potentially further \nwest into the Jackson Metro to Delta region where northerly winds \nlock in as the system is east-northeast of us in central AL into \nthe day Saturday. Tropical Storm Wind probs are in the low\ncategorical range (10 to 30 percent) but enough to support added\ntropical headlines. For this reason, a Tropical Storm Watch was \nadded for Forrest, Jones, Jasper, Newton, Neshoba, Winston, \nNoxubee, Kemper, Lauderdale and Clarke Counties through Saturday \nevening. Will continue to monitor for any potential in northward \nexpansion of the Tropical Storm Watch into Oktibbeha, Clay to \nLowndes County or any upgrades to a Warning. An Elevated was added\nearlier areawide to the HWO graphic for gusty winds, just due to \nthat better support in the CAMs and high- resolution ensemble wind\nprobs for gusty winds. \n\nFlash flooding is also possible starting Friday night through \nSaturday night. Expected totals are 2-4 inches of rain in north \ncentral, northeast to east MS while less in the 1-3 inch range in \nnorthwest, central to southeast MS. This is also due to warm cloud\nprocesses/tropical rain. As Isaias progress closer, additional \nupdates will be provided so please remain up to date with the \nlatest information.\n\nOnce the remnants of Isaias progress away from the area, hot and dry \nconditions will return as high pressure builds over the region.\n/DC/SW/",
